gn-In Design","\u002Fplatform\u002Freference\u002Fsign-in-design","03.platform\u002F12.reference\u002F04.sign-in-design",{"title":254,"path":255,"stem":256,"icon":257},"Development Server","\u002Fplatform\u002Fdevelopment-server","03.platform\u002F13.development-server","i-lucide-server-cog",{"title":259,"path":260,"stem":261,"icon":262},"Supply Chain Security","\u002Fplatform\u002Fsupply-chain-security","03.platform\u002F14.supply-chain-security","i-lucide-package-check",{"title":264,"path":265,"stem":266,"icon":267},"Enterprise License","\u002Fplatform\u002Fenterprise-license","03.platform\u002F15.enterprise-license","i-lucide-badge-check",{"id":269,"title":222,"body":270,"description":886,"extension":887,"links":888,"meta":889,"navigation":890,"path":223,"seo":891,"stem":224,"__hash__":892},"docs\u002F03.platform\u002F10.system-migrations.md",{"type":271,"value":272,"toc":873},"minimark",[273,282,287,290,302,306,314,318,321,337,342,345,398,412,517,520,535,541,545,560,570,574,588,618,634,642,662,680,727,741,752,786,804,834,850,854,869],[274,275,276,277,281],"p",{},"The Kinotic platform uses the same migration SQL system as application developers to manage its own internal Elasticsearch indices. This page covers considerations specific to platform development — for general migration syntax, see ",[278,279,280],"a",{"href":123},"App Migrations",".",[283,284,286],"h2",{"id":285},"what-are-system-migrations","What Are System Migrations?",[274,288,289],{},"System migrations define and evolve the indices that the platform itself relies on — things like migration history tracking, system configuration, and other internal data stores. They run during platform startup before any application migrations and are scoped to the system project rather than a per-application project.",[274,291,292,293,297,298,301],{},"System migrations live in ",[294,295,296],"code",{},"kinotic-migration\u002Fsrc\u002Fmain\u002Fresources\u002Fmigrations\u002F"," and follow the same ",[294,299,300],{},"V\u003CN>__\u003Cdescription>.sql"," naming convention.",[283,303,305],{"id":304},"using-composite-types-in-system-indices","Using Composite Types in System Indices",[274,307,308,309,313],{},"OBJECT, NESTED, and UNION columns work the same way in system migrations as in application migrations. Refer to the ",[278,310,312],{"href":311},"\u002Forganizations\u002Fapps\u002Fpersistence\u002Fmigrations#composite-column-types","Composite Column Types"," section of the app documentation for syntax and examples.",[283,315,317],{"id":316},"deserializing-union-types","Deserializing Union Types",[274,319,320],{},"Elasticsearch stores UNION fields as a flat merged object — it has no knowledge of which variant a document contains. When reading documents back into Java, the platform needs to route deserialization to the correct subclass.",[274,322,323,324,328,329,332,333,336],{},"The standard approach is Jackson's polymorphic type handling, driven by a ",[325,326,327],"strong",{},"discriminator field"," stored in the document. This is the same ",[294,330,331],{},"kind"," (or similar) ",[294,334,335],{},"KEYWORD"," field you include in each union variant in the migration SQL.",[338,339,341],"h3",{"id":340},"example","Example",[274,343,344],{},"Given this migration:",[346,347,352],"pre",{"className":348,"code":349,"language":350,"meta":351,"style":351},"language-sql shiki shiki-themes material-theme-lighter material-theme material-theme-palenight","CREATE TABLE assets (\n    id KEYWORD,\n    item UNION (\n        Book  (kind KEYWORD, title TEXT, isbn KEYWORD),\n        Video (kind KEYWORD, title TEXT, duration INTEGER)\n    )\n);\n","sql","",[294,353,354,362,368,374,380,386,392],{"__ignoreMap":351},[355,356,359],"span",{"class":357,"line":358},"line",1,[355,360,361],{},"CREATE TABLE assets (\n",[355,363,365],{"class":357,"line":364},2,[355,366,367],{},"    id KEYWORD,\n",[355,369,371],{"class":357,"line":370},3,[355,372,373],{},"    item UNION (\n",[355,375,377],{"class":357,"line":376},4,[355,378,379],{},"        Book  (kind KEYWORD, title TEXT, isbn KEYWORD),\n",[355,381,383],{"class":357,"line":382},5,[355,384,385],{},"        Video (kind KEYWORD, title TEXT, duration INTEGER)\n",[355,387,389],{"class":357,"line":388},6,[355,390,391],{},"    )\n",[355,393,395],{"class":357,"line":394},7,[355,396,397],{},");\n",[274,399,400,401,404,405,408,409,411],{},"The corresponding Java model uses ",[294,402,403],{},"@JsonTypeInfo"," and ",[294,406,407],{},"@JsonSubTypes"," to map the ",[294,410,331],{}," field value to the correct subclass:",[346,413,417],{"className":414,"code":415,"language":416,"meta":351,"style":351},"language-java shiki shiki-themes material-theme-lighter material-theme material-theme-palenight","@JsonTypeInfo(use = JsonTypeInfo.Id.NAME, property = \"kind\")\n@JsonSubTypes({\n    @JsonSubTypes.Type(value = Book.class,  name = \"Book\"),\n    @JsonSubTypes.Type(value = Video.class, name = \"Video\")\n})\npublic abstract class Item {}\n\npublic class Book extends Item {\n    public String kind;\n    public String title;\n    public String isbn;\n}\n\npublic class Video extends Item {\n    public String kind;\n    public String title;\n    public Integer duration;\n}\n","java",[294,418,419,424,429,434,439,444,449,455,461,467,473,479,485,490,496,501,506,512],{"__ignoreMap":351},[355,420,421],{"class":357,"line":358},[355,422,423],{},"@JsonTypeInfo(use = JsonTypeInfo.Id.NAME, property = \"kind\")\n",[355,425,426],{"class":357,"line":364},[355,427,428],{},"@JsonSubTypes({\n",[355,430,431],{"class":357,"line":370},[355,432,433],{},"    @JsonSubTypes.Type(value = Book.class,  name = \"Book\"),\n",[355,435,436],{"class":357,"line":376},[355,437,438],{},"    @JsonSubTypes.Type(value = Video.class, name = \"Video\")\n",[355,440,441],{"class":357,"line":382},[355,442,443],{},"})\n",[355,445,446],{"class":357,"line":388},[355,447,448],{},"public abstract class Item {}\n",[355,450,451],{"class":357,"line":394},[355,452,454],{"emptyLinePlaceholder":453},true,"\n",[355,456,458],{"class":357,"line":457},8,[355,459,460],{},"public class Book extends Item {\n",[355,462,464],{"class":357,"line":463},9,[355,465,466],{},"    public String kind;\n",[355,468,470],{"class":357,"line":469},10,[355,471,472],{},"    public String title;\n",[355,474,476],{"class":357,"line":475},11,[355,477,478],{},"    public String isbn;\n",[355,480,482],{"class":357,"line":481},12,[355,483,484],{},"}\n",[355,486,488],{"class":357,"line":487},13,[355,489,454],{"emptyLinePlaceholder":453},[355,491,493],{"class":357,"line":492},14,[355,494,495],{},"public class Video extends Item {\n",[355,497,499],{"class":357,"line":498},15,[355,500,466],{},[355,502,504],{"class":357,"line":503},16,[355,505,472],{},[355,507,509],{"class":357,"line":508},17,[355,510,511],{},"    public Integer duration;\n",[355,513,515],{"class":357,"line":514},18,[355,516,484],{},[274,518,519],{},"When inserting documents, always write the discriminator value:",[346,521,523],{"className":348,"code":522,"language":350,"meta":351,"style":351},"INSERT INTO assets (id, item)\n    VALUES ('a1', { kind: 'Book', title: 'Clean Code', isbn: '978-0132350884' }) WITH REFRESH;\n",[294,524,525,530],{"__ignoreMap":351},[355,526,527],{"class":357,"line":358},[355,528,529],{},"INSERT INTO assets (id, item)\n",[355,531,532],{"class":357,"line":364},[355,533,534],{},"    VALUES ('a1', { kind: 'Book', title: 'Clean Code', isbn: '978-0132350884' }) WITH REFRESH;\n",[274,536,537,538,540],{},"If the ",[294,539,331],{}," field is absent from a stored document, Jackson will fail to deserialize it. Ensure every INSERT that targets a UNION field includes the discriminator.",[338,542,544],{"id":543},"conflict-validation","Conflict validation",[274,546,547,548,551,552,555,556,559],{},"If two variants declare the same field name with different scalar types — e.g. ",[294,549,550],{},"title TEXT"," in one variant and ",[294,553,554],{},"title KEYWORD"," in another — the migration executor will throw an ",[294,557,558],{},"IllegalArgumentException"," at mapping time. Fields shared across variants must have identical types.",[274,561,562,565,566,569],{},[325,563,564],{},"Known limitation:"," Conflict detection is shallow for composite types. If two variants share a field name with the same composite type (OBJECT, NESTED, or UNION) but different sub-fields, no exception is thrown — only the first variant's sub-field definitions are used and the second's are silently dropped. Until named type support is added to the migration DSL, avoid sharing composite-typed fields across variants. Scalar shared fields (such as a ",[294,567,568],{},"kind KEYWORD"," discriminator) are always safe.",[283,571,573],{"id":572},"data-streams","Data Streams",[274,575,576,577,580,581,587],{},"System data that is append-only and time-series — audit trails, events, telemetry — should be backed by an Elasticsearch ",[325,578,579],{},"data stream"," rather than a regular index, so old data ages out and backing indices roll over automatically. Create one from a migration with ",[278,582,584],{"href":583},"\u002Forganizations\u002Fapps\u002Freference\u002Fmigration-sql-grammar#create-data-stream",[294,585,586],{},"CREATE DATA STREAM",":",[346,589,591],{"className":348,"code":590,"language":350,"meta":351,"style":351},"CREATE DATA STREAM system_audit (\n    actor  KEYWORD,\n    action KEYWORD,\n    detail TEXT\n) WITH (DATA_RETENTION = '90d') ;\n",[294,592,593,598,603,608,613],{"__ignoreMap":351},[355,594,595],{"class":357,"line":358},[355,596,597],{},"CREATE DATA STREAM system_audit (\n",[355,599,600],{"class":357,"line":364},[355,601,602],{},"    actor  KEYWORD,\n",[355,604,605],{"class":357,"line":370},[355,606,607],{},"    action KEYWORD,\n",[355,609,610],{"class":357,"line":376},[355,611,612],{},"    detail TEXT\n",[355,614,615],{"class":357,"line":382},[355,616,617],{},") WITH (DATA_RETENTION = '90d') ;\n",[274,619,620,623,624,630,631,633],{},[294,621,622],{},"DATA_RETENTION"," is the native ",[278,625,629],{"href":626,"rel":627},"https:\u002F\u002Fwww.elastic.co\u002Fdocs\u002Fmanage-data\u002Flifecycle\u002Fdata-stream",[628],"nofollow","data stream lifecycle"," — Elasticsearch deletes data past the period (measured per backing index from rollover, not per document). See the ",[278,632,171],{"href":583}," for the full statement and the exact retention semantics.",[338,635,637,638,641],{"id":636},"every-document-needs-a-timestamp-and-java-needs-ceremony-for-it","Every document needs a ",[294,639,640],{},"@timestamp"," — and Java needs ceremony for it",[274,643,644,645,648,649,651,652,655,656,658,659,661],{},"Elasticsearch ",[325,646,647],{},"requires"," every document written to a data stream to carry a ",[294,650,640],{}," date field, and rejects any that don't with ",[294,653,654],{},"data stream timestamp field [@timestamp] is missing",". The migration adds ",[294,657,640],{}," to the mapping automatically — you neither declare it as a column nor can you, since ",[294,660,640],{}," is not a legal column identifier.",[274,663,664,665,668,669,671,672,675,676,679],{},"The catch is on the ",[325,666,667],{},"write"," side: ",[294,670,640],{}," is also not a legal Java field name, so a system DTO cannot carry it directly. Bridge it with Jackson's ",[294,673,674],{},"@JsonProperty"," — the ",[294,677,678],{},"ElasticsearchAsyncClient"," serializes through a Jackson mapper, so the annotation is honored on both write and read:",[346,681,683],{"className":414,"code":682,"language":416,"meta":351,"style":351},"public class AuditEvent {\n    private String  id;\n\n    @JsonProperty(\"@timestamp\")      \u002F\u002F Java field `timestamp` ⇄ Elasticsearch field `@timestamp`\n    private Instant timestamp;\n\n    private String  actor;\n    private String  action;\n}\n",[294,684,685,690,695,699,704,709,713,718,723],{"__ignoreMap":351},[355,686,687],{"class":357,"line":358},[355,688,689],{},"public class AuditEvent {\n",[355,691,692],{"class":357,"line":364},[355,693,694],{},"    private String  id;\n",[355,696,697],{"class":357,"line":370},[355,698,454],{"emptyLinePlaceholder":453},[355,700,701],{"class":357,"line":376},[355,702,703],{},"    @JsonProperty(\"@timestamp\")      \u002F\u002F Java field `timestamp` ⇄ Elasticsearch field `@timestamp`\n",[355,705,706],{"class":357,"line":382},[355,707,708],{},"    private Instant timestamp;\n",[355,710,711],{"class":357,"line":388},[355,712,454],{"emptyLinePlaceholder":453},[355,714,715],{"class":357,"line":394},[355,716,717],{},"    private String  actor;\n",[355,719,720],{"class":357,"line":457},[355,721,722],{},"    private String  action;\n",[355,724,725],{"class":357,"line":463},[355,726,484],{},[274,728,729,730,733,734,736,737,740],{},"This ",[325,731,732],{},"renames"," the field — the stored document has only ",[294,735,640],{},", so any query or sort must use that literal name (",[294,738,739],{},"timestamp"," does not exist in the index). For most internal stream DTOs this is the simplest correct choice.",[274,742,743,744,748,749,751],{},"If you instead want a friendly, separately-queryable field name ",[745,746,747],"em",{},"alongside"," ",[294,750,640],{},", keep your own field and add a serialize-only companion:",[346,753,755],{"className":414,"code":754,"language":416,"meta":351,"style":351},"@JsonProperty(\"eventTime\")           \u002F\u002F stays queryable as eventTime\nprivate Instant eventTime;\n\n\u002F\u002F emitted as @timestamp on write, ignored on read (eventTime is restored from its own key)\n@JsonProperty(value = \"@timestamp\", access = JsonProperty.Access.READ_ONLY)\npublic Instant esTimestamp() { return eventTime; }\n",[294,756,757,762,767,771,776,781],{"__ignoreMap":351},[355,758,759],{"class":357,"line":358},[355,760,761],{},"@JsonProperty(\"eventTime\")           \u002F\u002F stays queryable as eventTime\n",[355,763,764],{"class":357,"line":364},[355,765,766],{},"private Instant eventTime;\n",[355,768,769],{"class":357,"line":370},[355,770,454],{"emptyLinePlaceholder":453},[355,772,773],{"class":357,"line":376},[355,774,775],{},"\u002F\u002F emitted as @timestamp on write, ignored on read (eventTime is restored from its own key)\n",[355,777,778],{"class":357,"line":382},[355,779,780],{},"@JsonProperty(value = \"@timestamp\", access = JsonProperty.Access.READ_ONLY)\n",[355,782,783],{"class":357,"line":388},[355,784,785],{},"public Instant esTimestamp() { return eventTime; }\n",[274,787,729,788,791,792,404,795,797,798,803],{},[325,789,790],{},"duplicates"," the value (both ",[294,793,794],{},"eventTime",[294,796,640],{}," are stored). It is the same document shape that application entities get from the ",[278,799,800],{"href":107},[294,801,802],{},"@TimeReference"," decorator. When a migration creates the stream backing such an entity, declare the pairing so the stream documents which column is the natural time field:",[346,805,807],{"className":348,"code":806,"language":350,"meta":351,"style":351},"CREATE DATA STREAM sensor_readings (\n    id        KEYWORD,\n    eventTime DATE,\n    value     DOUBLE\n) WITH (DATA_RETENTION = '30d', TIME_REFERENCE = 'eventTime') ;\n",[294,808,809,814,819,824,829],{"__ignoreMap":351},[355,810,811],{"class":357,"line":358},[355,812,813],{},"CREATE DATA STREAM sensor_readings (\n",[355,815,816],{"class":357,"line":364},[355,817,818],{},"    id        KEYWORD,\n",[355,820,821],{"class":357,"line":370},[355,822,823],{},"    eventTime DATE,\n",[355,825,826],{"class":357,"line":376},[355,827,828],{},"    value     DOUBLE\n",[355,830,831],{"class":357,"line":382},[355,832,833],{},") WITH (DATA_RETENTION = '30d', TIME_REFERENCE = 'eventTime') ;\n",[274,835,836,839,840,843,844,846,847,849],{},[294,837,838],{},"TIME_REFERENCE"," must name a declared ",[294,841,842],{},"DATE"," column; the migration validates this and fails fast otherwise. It declares intent and pairs with the entity's ",[294,845,802],{}," — the write-time duplication into ",[294,848,640],{}," is performed by the persistence layer, not the migration.",[338,851,853],{"id":852},"writing-and-reading","Writing and reading",[274,855,856,857,860,861,864,865,868],{},"Data streams are ",[325,858,859],{},"append-only",": Elasticsearch accepts only the ",[294,862,863],{},"create"," op-type and rejects the ",[294,866,867],{},"index"," op and by-id updates\u002Fdeletes. Documents are retrieved by search\u002Fquery, not by id. System services that write to a stream must therefore use a create (append) write, and read through search rather than a find-by-id lookup.",[870,871,872],"style",{},"html .light .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-light);background: var(--shiki-light-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-light-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-light-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-light-text-decoration);}html.light .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-light);background: var(--shiki-light-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-light-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-light-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-light-text-decoration);}html .default .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-default);background: var(--shiki-default-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-default-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-default-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-default-text-decoration);}html .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-default);background: var(--shiki-default-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-default-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-default-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-default-text-decoration);}html .dark .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-dark);background: var(--shiki-dark-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-dark-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-dark-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-dark-text-decoration);}html.dark .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-dark);background: var(--shiki-dark-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-dark-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-dark-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-dark-text-decoration);}",{"title":351,"searchDepth":364,"depth":364,"links":874},[875,876,877,881],{"id":285,"depth":364,"text":286},{"id":304,"depth":364,"text":305},{"id":316,"depth":364,"text":317,"children":878},[879,880],{"id":340,"depth":370,"text":341},{"id":543,"depth":370,"text":544},{"id":572,"depth":364,"text":573,"children":882},[883,885],{"id":636,"depth":370,"text":884},"Every document needs a @timestamp — and Java needs ceremony for it",{"id":852,"depth":370,"text":853},"Writing and maintaining migration scripts for Kinotic's internal system indices.","md",null,{},{"icon":225},{"title":222,"description":886},"hnVzDae1-PGhOgUplqr6vEsDbapSNl9ITi7-Kq-dpEw",[894,896],{"title":217,"path":218,"stem":219,"description":895,"icon":220,"children":-1},"How to contribute to the Kinotic OS project.",{"title":227,"path":228,"stem":229,"description":897,"icon":230,"children":-1},"How a call through a service proxy fails when the node serving it dies, instead of waiting forever — without a timeout anywhere.",1792930936789]